              "explanation": "*\"Hydrated zeolites are used as ion exchangers in softening of 'hard' water.\"*\n\n---\n\n## MASTER FINAL AUDIT OF REMAINING FACTS & COMPREHENSIVE CHAPTER SUMMARY\n\n### Comprehensive Audit of Minor NCERT Details Covered Across All 6 Sections\n\n1. **Boron Isotopic Abundance & Atomic Mass:** ¹⁰B = 19%, ¹¹B = 81%, Atomic mass = 10.81 g/mol.\n2. **Abundance Ranks in Earth's Crust:** Oxygen = 1st (45.5%), Silicon = 2nd (27.7%), Aluminium = 3rd / 1st metal (8.3%), Carbon = 17th.\n3. **Borax Mineral Formula & Indian Locations:** Na₂B₄O₇ · 10H₂O; Found in Puga Valley (Ladakh) and Sambhar Lake (Rajasthan).\n4. **Kernite & Cryolite Formulas:** Kernite = Na₂B₄O₇ · 4H₂O; Cryolite = Na₃AlF₆.\n5. **Atomic Radius Anomaly:** Gallium (135 pm) < Aluminium (143 pm) due to poor screening by 10 d-electrons.\n6. **Ionization Enthalpy Discontinuities:** Discontinuities between Al and Ga, and between In and Tl are due to poor shielding of intervening d and f electrons.\n7. **Gallium Liquid Range:** MP = 303 K, BP = 2676 K; liquid during summer, used in high-temperature thermometers.\n8. **Oxidation State Stability Trends:** Group 13: +1 state stability increases Al < Ga < In < Tl (Tl⁺ predominant, Tl³⁺ oxidising). Group 14: +2 state stability increases Ge < Sn < Pb (Sn²⁺ reducing, Pb⁴⁺ oxidising).\n9. **Halogen-Bridged Dimer Al₂Cl₆ Structure:** Terminal Al–Cl = 206 pm, Bridge Al–Cl = 221 pm, Bridge angle Cl–Al–Cl = 79°, Terminal angle Cl–Al–Cl = 118°; sp³ hybridised Al.\n10. **Hydrates and Hydrolysis of Trichlorides:** Form tetrahedral [M(OH)₄]⁻ (sp³) in neutral water, and octahedral [Al(H₂O)₆]³⁺ (sp³d²) in acid.\n11. **Reactivity towards Air & Passivity:** Crystalline B is unreactive; Amorphous B and Al form oxides B₂O₃/Al₂O₃ and nitrides BN/AlN at high temperature; Conc. HNO₃ renders Al passive.\n12. **Borax Hydrolysis & Borax Bead Test:** Borax in water forms alkaline solution (NaOH + H₃BO₃); heating with CoO forms blue Cobalt metaborate bead, Co(BO₂)₂.\n13. **Orthoboric Acid Properties & Structure:** White crystalline solid, soapy touch, sparingly soluble in cold water, highly soluble in hot water; weak Lewis acid B(OH)₃ + 2H₂O ⇌ [B(OH)₄]⁻ + H₃O⁺; 2D layer lattice joined by hydrogen bonds; decomposes above 370 K to HBO₂ and then B₂O₃.\n14. **Diborane Synthesis & Reactions:** Lab: 2NaBH₄ + I₂ → B₂H₆ + 2NaI + H₂; Industrial: 2BF₃ + 6NaH --(450 K)--> B₂H₆ + 6NaF; Ether: 4BF₃ + 3LiAlH₄ → 2B₂H₆ + 3LiF + 3AlF₃; Δc H° = -1976 kJ/mol; Reacts with CO to give BH₃ · CO; with NH₃ gives [BH₂(NH₃)₂]⁺[BH₄]⁻ which upon heating yields borazine B₃N₃H₆ (inorganic benzene).\n15. **Diborane Structural Metrics:** Terminal B–H = 119 pm (2c-2e), Bridge B–H = 134 pm (3c-2e / banana bond), Terminal H–B–H angle = 120°, Bridge B–H–B angle = 97°; sp³ hybridised B.\n16. **Group 14 Isotope & Allotropes:** Carbon isotopes: ¹²C, ¹³C (stable), ¹⁴C (radioactive, half-life = 5770 yrs); Catenation order: C >> Si > Ge ≈ Sn (Pb none); Bond enthalpy C–C = 348 kJ/mol.\n17. **Diamond vs Graphite Metrics:** Diamond: sp³, C–C = 154 pm, density = 3.51 g/cm³; Graphite: sp², C–C intra-layer = 141.5 pm, inter-layer = 340 pm, density = 2.22 g/cm³.\n18. **Fullerenes (C₆₀ Buckminsterfullerene):** 20 six-membered and 12 five-membered rings; 60 vertices; 5-membered rings fuse ONLY with 6-membered rings; C–C single = 143.5 pm, C=C double = 138.3 pm; Δf H°(C₆₀) = 38.1 kJ/mol, Δf H°(diamond) = 1.90 kJ/mol, Δf H°(graphite) = 0.\n19. **Carbon Monoxide & Dioxide:** Pure CO prepared by HCOOH dehydration with conc. H₂SO₄ at 373 K; Water gas = CO + H₂ (473–1273 K); Producer gas = CO + N₂ (1273 K); CO forms haemoglobin complex 300x more stable than O₂; Blood pH buffer H₂CO₃/HCO₃⁻ = 7.26–7.42; CO₂ linear, C–O = 115 pm, dipole moment = 0.\n20. **Silica (SiO₂):** 95% of earth's crust; Quartz, cristobalite, tridymite (crystalline); Resists acids except HF (forms SiF₄) and alkalis (forms Na₂SiO₃); 8-membered alternate Si-O rings.\n21. **Silicones:** Repeating unit (R₂SiO)ₙ; CH₃Cl + Si --(Cu, 573 K)--> Me₂SiCl₂ (linear), CH₃SiCl₃ (cross-linked), (CH₃)₃SiCl (chain end-blocker); hydrophobic, high thermal stability, biocompatible.\n22. **Silicates & Zeolites:** Structural unit SiO₄⁴⁻ (tetrahedral); 3D framework replacement of Si⁴⁺ by Al³⁺ creates negative aluminosilicate balanced by Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺; ZSM-5 converts alcohols directly to gasoline; hydrated zeolites act as ion exchangers in water softening.",
